before in the podcast.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:36.970
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.820
We have Melanie Warrick
joining us today.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.060
She's going to talk to
us about what is AI?

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.260
What does that mean?

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.570
What does that relate
to machine learning?

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:47.830
Is it just a brand new thing?

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:49.150
Is it something just different?

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:49.674
Who knows?

00:00:49.674 --> 00:00:50.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.540
I really like it because
this episode came up

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.094
after basically
having lunch, and we

00:00:56.094 --> 00:00:58.510
were talking about machine
learning and AI and everything.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.997
And she was like, well,
AI and machine learning

00:01:00.997 --> 00:01:02.080
are very different things.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.706
And we're like, really?

00:01:03.706 --> 00:01:04.830
And she had that opinions--

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:05.269
MARK MANDEL: Yes, tell us more.

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:06.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
she has opinions of this.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.230
So we decided to make an
episode out of her opinions.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:10.813
And they're really
interesting, so I'm

00:01:10.813 --> 00:01:12.355
very excited about
the whole episode.

00:01:12.355 --> 00:01:13.063
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:01:13.063 --> 00:01:15.280
And then after that, we
have our usual question

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.990
of the week where we talk about,
say you want to do something

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.400
whenever any pod within
your Kubernetes cluster

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.440
is either created or destroyed.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:24.310
How do you do that?

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:25.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.080
I have some ideas.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.080
But before we go to
that and before we

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.960
go with the main content, we
have as always our cool things

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:32.960
of the week.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.260
And we have four of them today.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.610
So I'm going to let you start.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:37.360
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.740
So the first one here is
around Stackdriver Logging.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.645
It went GA a little
while ago, but we've

00:01:42.645 --> 00:01:44.770
been basically talking to
a bunch of the people who

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:45.790
use this product.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.800
And we want to give them
some new features that

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:48.574
are super cool.

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:49.740
So there's a bunch of stuff.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.230
We'll link to the full articles.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.646
Actually, there's a couple
that come out of the blog post.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:56.500
But you can do neat stuff like
being able to pull metrics out

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:57.250
of logging now.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.590
So if you're tracking
response times

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:02.380
or any other types of
metrics, you can pull that in.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.430
We've made it faster to make log
entries available within there.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.669
You can manage logs
across organizations.

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:11.170
So not only just managing
logs within a project,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.210
you can do log
aggregation exports

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.475
across wide varieties of
projects all together.

00:02:15.475 --> 00:02:18.100
And you can also do this really
cool thing which I quite like--

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.350
and we'll link to a
longer article about it--

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.017
which is now called exclusion
filters, which is say,

00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:24.850
for example, you might
have debug statements

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.400
that you have that are spitting
out from your applications,

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.500
but most of the time you
actually don't care about them.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.910
So you can say,
hey, I don't really

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.350
care about these
with Stackdriver,

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:33.891
so that means it's
not going to count

00:02:33.891 --> 00:02:36.580
towards the gigabytes
or the storage costs

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:40.600
that you would necessarily
have there within your project.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.270
Which is probably good to know,
because Stackdriver Logging now

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:47.050
offers 50 gigabytes of logs
per project per month for free.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:48.850
That will be starting
in December 1.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.240
I think most people will
probably not hit that 50

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.730
gigabytes, but some will.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.060
So be aware that that is coming.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.697
But yeah, so up to 50 gigabytes.

00:02:56.697 --> 00:02:59.030
But yeah, you can use exclusion
filters, pull that down.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:00.821
Maybe if you're getting
like health checks,

00:03:00.821 --> 00:03:03.310
you're like, hmm, don't care
about those, 200 requests,

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:04.190
stuff like that.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:05.980
There's a longer
conversation about that.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:07.900
One of our other
teammates, Aja Hammerly,

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.700
who wrote a whole
article about preventing

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.190
log waste with
Stackdriver Logging

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.524
that we'll also link to as well.

00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:13.648
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:03:13.648 --> 00:03:15.460
50 gigabytes sounds
like a lot, but I

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.800
guess that some more customers
might be somewhere around

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.300
there--

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:18.640
MARK MANDEL: Definitely.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:19.931
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --even higher.

00:03:19.931 --> 00:03:20.650
So good to know.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:24.430
The second cool thing of
the week is a new article.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.970
It's actually the
third part of a trilogy

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:31.360
on how to build conversational
applications using

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.760
Cloud Machine Learning APIs.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.860
And it is really cool,
partly because I'm actually

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.370
preparing a talk for Sydney.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.250
I will be talking
exactly about this topic.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.000
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:40.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So it's very good

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.940
to see that there's
a new article on it.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.460
And basically, it's
about how do you

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.160
describe intents for Google--

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:48.952
not Google Home.

00:03:48.952 --> 00:03:51.160
The example is with Google
Home, but you can actually

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:52.580
do with whatever you want to do.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:53.955
Actions with Google
Home, but you

00:03:53.955 --> 00:03:57.670
can also do things like
Facebook Messenger or Slack.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.370
And all of these things
work through API.AI.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.900
And it is very cool because it
is actually incredibly simple

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:04.400
to use.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.880
You basically say,
OK, if someone says,

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.730
I'd like to buy
some milk, then you

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:11.560
know that you're
going to relate that

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:15.040
to a use case, which is
the buy, buy something,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.779
and the product is milk.

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:18.649
And then you can
make it to be like,

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:20.267
oh, well, we don't
know the quantity,

00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:21.350
so let's ask the quantity.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.710
So you can actually create
this two-ways conversation

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.360
that then you can
use from any device.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.850
So you write it only once.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:30.790
You're write you're
backend on the other side.

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:34.490
And then people will be able
to access your smart service

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.100
from, as I was saying, Facebook
Messenger, Slack, Google Home,

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:43.100
but also like Skype and
LINE and way more services.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:43.810
It's pretty cool.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:44.410
MARK MANDEL: That is cool.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.360
Well, I can't wait to
see your talk, Francesc.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.865
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I can't
