Mark and I met at
Strange Loop actually.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.750
MARK MANDEL: A long,
long, long time ago,

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:32.980
in a land far away, but yes.

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:34.450
I got very excited
when I found out

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:35.460
Melanie was joining the team.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:36.460
MELANIE WARRICK: I know.

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:37.675
Me too.

00:09:37.675 --> 00:09:38.550
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:39.049
All right.

00:09:39.049 --> 00:09:42.420
Well, we're here
to talk about AI.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.760
So what is AI?

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.687
Is it ML just like a new coat
of paint, or what's the deal?

00:09:47.687 --> 00:09:49.770
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
what it feels like lately.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.990
Everything's called AI.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:53.550
Artificial intelligence
in particular

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:57.180
is, the actual definition is
the science and engineering

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.850
of technology or tech in
some way, shape, or form.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.077
And this was initially
coined back in the '50s

00:10:03.077 --> 00:10:04.660
to give us a history
lesson, because I

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:06.368
know that's what
everybody wants to hear.

00:10:06.368 --> 00:10:10.510
But that's when it
originally was labeled.

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:13.900
And then, over the years,
it's been this process

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.520
of seeing if we can
actually achieve

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:16.750
what people expect from it.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.880
But the reality is the
expectations are constantly

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:20.980
kind of changing, and
what people really

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:23.080
want when they say
AI, a lot of people

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:25.810
are really just looking
for human-like artificial

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:26.470
intelligence.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.635
They want to see robots
that are like humans.

00:10:28.635 --> 00:10:33.490
It's what we have fun with
all of our sci-fi and fantasy.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:36.370
When we see it labeled
today, most people

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:38.560
are talking about what's
known as narrow AI,

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:41.680
and so they're
expecting technology

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.280
that's really good in a
specific area, that's constantly

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:46.680
learning and adapting.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:48.430
So that's where that
term is getting used,

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:53.890
and it's replacing a lot of
times ML, or Machine Learning.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.354
Machine learning, in
essence, is the algorithms

00:10:56.354 --> 00:10:58.270
that are used in AI, or
some of the algorithms

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.103
that are used in AI.

00:10:59.103 --> 00:11:01.240
And when I say algorithms,
I mean the math part,

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:03.850
stuff that you use
the computations for.

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you
mentioned narrow intelligence.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:08.430
I can imagine like,
for instance, there

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.750
was an example that we
talked about as a cool thing

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:14.100
of the week where there
was this farm of cucumbers.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:16.690
And there was something
that was able to decide

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:19.390
the kind of cucumber
that it was able to see.

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:20.557
That would be narrow, right?

00:11:20.557 --> 00:11:21.515
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:11:21.515 --> 00:11:23.530
So what you're talking
about in that cucumber

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:26.140
example in particular,
that is using

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.980
an algorithm that is known as
deep learning or neural nets.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:34.620
And in particular, that's a type
of machine learning algorithm.

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:36.760
And machine learning
is a set of algorithms

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:39.352
that are used in
artificial intelligence.

00:11:39.352 --> 00:11:40.810
But it's not the
only thing you use

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:42.340
for artificial intelligence.

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:44.480
And so for the
cucumber example--

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:47.680
and I'd have to look at it to
really make sure I'm on point

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.660
on this-- but basically
the cucumber example

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:52.420
is machine learning for sure.

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:55.930
And if it's continually
adapting and changing

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.150
in terms of how
it operates, then

00:11:58.150 --> 00:11:59.680
technically it's narrow AI.

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:01.430
Now, some would say,
well, it's definitely

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:05.380
AI based on this current
paradigm we're living in.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:10.360
But I would say for narrow
AI, it's probably there,

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:12.280
but it just depends
on how much it

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:14.180
continues to change and learn.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:14.930
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.580
And you said something
before that I thought

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:17.705
it was kind of interesting.

00:12:17.705 --> 00:12:20.320
You said when people come
to AI, what they often want

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:23.150
is a human-like response.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:25.175
But when you're talking
about sort of narrow AI,

00:12:25.175 --> 00:12:28.000
I'm almost wondering whether
the response you get out

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.580
of machines is almost things
that possibly humans couldn't

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:31.392
do?

00:12:31.392 --> 00:12:32.350
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:34.830
So when I say when
they come to AI

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.880
and they want
human-like response,

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:39.220
they want to see a
robot that really can

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:41.700
think and feel and have ideas.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.180
And you have to
say, when you look

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:45.850
at a car that can
drive itself, does it

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:47.620
have an awareness of itself?

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.060
Is it conscious?

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:51.010
Can generalize not
just from driving

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:53.710
to actually doing other
things that are out there?

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:56.050
And granted, it's pretty
general to be able to drive.

00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:59.290
But in terms of narrow, you
have to think about the fact

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:01.930
that it's able to
specifically be

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:05.830
an expert in a specific
field, and it could be better

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:07.840
than humans in that field.

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:12.200
AlphaGo and DeepMind
had successfully

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:16.210
beat a number of the best
players in that field.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:19.300
That is a great example of
where you have an algorithm that

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:22.360
has been successfully
trained to exceed

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:24.580
what humans are capable of.

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:27.287
So that's definitely
very possible.

00:13:27.287 --> 00:13:29.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
that would be narrow AI.

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:31.732
And for general AI or wide AI--

00:13:31.732 --> 00:13:32.940
I don't know how to call it--

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:36.580
other than "Prometheus" and
"Alien" and movies like that,

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:40.409
is there an actual use
case of that kind of AI?

00:13:40.409 --> 00:13:41.950
MELANIE WARRICK: Do
we have that now?

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:43.510
As far as everybody knows, no.

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.081
I mean, maybe somebody is
hiding something in a back room

00:13:46.081 --> 00:13:46.580
somewhere.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:49.540
But as far as we all know,
all we have right now

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.100
is what's termed as narrow AI.

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:53.230
We don't actually
have general AI,

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:54.850
which is what you
usually mean when

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:56.890
you're talking about
anything that's human-like.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:00.460
And then there's super
AI because you've

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:04.420
got to have the super with
all the various Superman,

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:05.848
super shows that are coming out.

00:14:05.848 --> 00:14:06.806
MARK MANDEL: All right.

00:14:06.806 --> 00:14:07.720
Now I'm excited.

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:08.720
MELANIE WARRICK: I know.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.020
So this is where you
start to get down to where

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:12.240
they talk about singularity.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.350
And they say this
is having AI that

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:18.080
will go above and beyond what
humans can actually understand.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.260
It's capable of
doing things that

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:22.390
go beyond our comprehension.

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:26.950
And that's where you start to
dive into the real extreme.

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:31.340
The world will come to an
end, or we will live forever.

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:33.940
And there's some
real serious concerns

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:35.980
in this space for
certain people.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:41.350
It gets used, especially in
the fun, sci-fi fantasy world.

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:43.474
But the realities of
that, it's not really

00:14:43.474 --> 00:14:45.640
something that the people
who are really researching

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:47.265
the space are concerned
about, and they

00:14:47.265 --> 00:14:50.814
don't see it as being a reality
if ever, if anytime soon.

00:14:50.814 --> 00:14:51.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:55.300
We'll come back to those
concerns a little bit later,

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:58.180
but you mentioned something that
I thought was very interesting.

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:00.526
You said that
machine learning is

00:15:00.526 --> 00:15:05.730
the algorithms that are used for
AI, but AI is more than machine

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:06.231
learning.

00:15:06.231 --> 00:15:07.188
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:15:07.188 --> 00:15:09.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
else do we have in AI

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:11.100
that is not machine learning?

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:12.600
MELANIE WARRICK:
So machine learning

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:14.280
is a field of
research in the space,

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:15.988
and there's a number
of different fields.

00:15:15.988 --> 00:15:17.970
You have statistics
and probability models

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:20.050
that get intermixed
with machine learning,

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:21.730
but they are their own thing.

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:25.030
They're not necessarily
machine learning.

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:26.670
You have graph theory.

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:30.030
Some people might even
touch on quantum mechanics

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:33.270
or quantum computing, which
is also a little bit more

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:36.370
of a not fully-concrete
space itself.

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:38.730
But it's fun to
explore and think

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:40.480
about what's the
possibility there

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:44.102
and how it can potentially have
impact in AI at some point.

00:15:44.102 --> 00:15:45.810
So there are a number
of different fields

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:49.030
that will come together,
sometimes on their own,

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:52.410
sometimes in concert,
to be able to achieve

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:54.100
this artificial intelligence.

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:54.850
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:57.120
So probably bring
it back to, say,

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.720
like Google Cloud or some
of our open source projects,

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:02.880
is TensorFlow AI, or
is it machine learning,

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:05.190
or does it just depend
on how you use the thing?

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:07.710
MELANIE WARRICK: So TensorFlow
primarily gets referenced

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:09.960
and used in relation
to deep learning.

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.880
And as I mentioned,
that's where you

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:14.760
start talking about neural nets
and applying neural nets, which

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:16.920
are one of many
algorithms that are

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:18.670
in the machine learning space.

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:21.330
So yes, it can play
a role and be used

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:25.920
and has been used in artificial
intelligence projects.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.890
DeepMind, for example,
currently uses

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.600
TensorFlow in some
of their algorithms

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:32.409
when they're doing
anything around gaming.

00:16:32.409 --> 00:16:34.950
Not everything, but I know that
it's used in some of the work

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:37.050
that they're doing
and other research

00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:38.250
spaces that they're in.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:39.750
Just because you're
using TensorFlow

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:40.920
doesn't mean you're doing AI.

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:41.836
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:41.836 --> 00:16:45.450
I mean, the Hello World of
TensorFlow is you do 2 plus 2,

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:47.130
and you run it and it says 4.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:48.337
That's probably not AI.

00:16:48.337 --> 00:16:49.670
MELANIE WARRICK: No, that's not.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:50.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:52.915
MELANIE WARRICK: But yeah,
it's definitely deep learning,

00:16:52.915 --> 00:16:55.980
or it can be deep learning,
and primarily that

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.694
is machine learning as
the bigger umbrella.

00:16:58.694 --> 00:16:59.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:02.310
So since you're a
developer advocating--

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:06.000
your main topic is
AI, machine learning.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.109
Could you tell us a little
bit about what kind of things

00:17:09.109 --> 00:17:12.300
can you do with AI
or machine learning

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.645
on Google Cloud Platform, or
what do people tend to do?

00:17:15.645 --> 00:17:17.190
What do you see the most often?

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:19.190
MELANIE WARRICK: So in
terms of machine learning

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:22.050
on Google Cloud Platform,
Google Cloud Platform

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:23.308
it's basically servers.

00:17:23.308 --> 00:17:24.849
It's computers at
the end of the day.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:27.660
So you can do whatever you
want that you currently can

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.050
do on Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:30.050 --> 00:17:32.580
That's something that I think
is really important to stress,

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:36.240
that it's like any cloud
platform you're going to use.

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:39.220
You can use the tools,
any tools you want.

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:40.470
You just have to configure it.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:42.053
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
one of those tools

00:17:42.053 --> 00:17:44.130
may be like, for
instance, TensorFlow,

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:47.540
for which we have a
managed version, which

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:49.512
is Cloud ML Engine.

00:17:49.512 --> 00:17:50.970
MARK MANDEL: What
is it called now?

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:51.886
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:17:51.886 --> 00:17:53.180
That's my knowledge as well.

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:55.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
it's Cloud ML Engine.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:58.260
But if you were interested
in using something

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:00.450
else other than TensorFlow,
you could also use it.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:00.930
MELANIE WARRICK: Correct.

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:02.346
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Now, the thing is

00:18:02.346 --> 00:18:04.650
that I have no idea what
other things are out there

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:05.670
other than TensorFlow.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:06.560
So I don't know.

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:10.180
Is there's some other
very well-known library,

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:12.600
some competitor of TensorFlow
that you've seen before

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:13.740
and that people use it?

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:14.698
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:18:14.698 --> 00:18:17.820
So Keras gets referenced a
lot because it's a great way

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:21.210
to engage with TensorFlow or
with other neural net platforms

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:22.460
that exist out there.

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:28.870
It's nice, higher-level abstract
to engage with that space.

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:29.632
So Keras is great.

00:18:29.632 --> 00:18:31.840
You can definitely use that
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:34.960
You just to configure the actual
servers to be able to use it.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:37.530
There are other libraries
that are constantly

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:41.280
being referenced, like
PyTorch and Caffe.

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:42.780
There's definitely
a few others that

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.820
are out there that I
would recommend exploring

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.760
because each one has
its own value in terms

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:49.150
of how it's being applied.

00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:51.690
MARK MANDEL: So I know
with Cloud ML Engine,

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:53.670
the TensorFlow setup can be--

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.560
the calculations
can be distributed

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.480
across multiple computers.

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:59.190
If you're coming
to GCP and using

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:03.420
something else like PyTorch
or something like that,

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:07.620
do those come with tools that
also will enable you to set up

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:10.180
a multitude of computers so you
can distribute that workload?

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:11.680
Or how does that
kind of thing work?

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:12.030
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:13.540
And I know we were talking
about this earlier.

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:14.940
So I wrote a blog
post about this

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:17.440
recently because I wanted to
make sure it is clear to people

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:19.800
that TensorFlow
is very valuable,

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:21.660
and I recommend
people exploring that.

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.280
But if you are already
using something

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.969
and you want to be able to
use Google Cloud Platform,

00:19:25.969 --> 00:19:28.260
I think is important to
understand that whatever you're

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:30.114
using you can bring over.

00:19:30.114 --> 00:19:31.530
So I wrote this
post in particular

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:34.890
to show you have to spend some
time doing the configuration.

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:38.800
If you already have an install
script or some type of docker

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:41.520
image that you use to
configure your instances,

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:42.760
then you can use that.

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:45.135
You don't have to spend the
time doing the configuration.

00:19:45.135 --> 00:19:48.977
You just kick off whatever you
need using Kubernetes, using

00:19:48.977 --> 00:19:51.060
whatever it is that will
help get those servers up

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:52.330
and running for you.

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.020
You just have to have some way
of doing the configuration,

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:57.170
and then you can bring
over your software.

00:19:57.170 --> 00:19:59.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So when using
that software, as soon as Mark

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:03.330
said big scale and
the cloud and stuff,

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:05.469
I started thinking
about big data.

00:20:05.469 --> 00:20:07.760
And I'm curious about the
relationship between big data

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:09.680
and things like Spark
and [? MapThus ?]

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:13.070
and stuff like that and
what we talk about machine

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:14.640
learning and AI.

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.960
Is AI part of big data?

00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:19.520
Is big data part of AI?

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:20.940
What are the
relationships there?

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:22.315
MELANIE WARRICK:
The relationship

00:20:22.315 --> 00:20:25.220
between big data and AI is that,
in some of these algorithms

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:28.310
and many of the algorithms,
the data will actually

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:30.500
help build them and
make them successful

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:33.020
and make them actually work
well, especially when you

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:34.400
get to neural nets.

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:37.730
So big data is about
having the data

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:40.260
you need to train the model,
and that's what it's all about.

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:42.740
I mean, frankly,
most of the work

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:44.750
you do when you're
building these models is

00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:49.217
getting the data and having the
data cleaned and ready to go.

00:20:49.217 --> 00:20:51.050
Something I like to
tell people all the time

00:20:51.050 --> 00:20:52.990
is never trust the data.

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:56.180
Data needs to be fully vetted
and cleaned and processed,

00:20:56.180 --> 00:20:59.070
and like 80% of your time
can be spent on that.

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:01.790
But you do have to spend
the time figuring out

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:04.070
what it is you
have, what you need,

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:08.360
and getting it into a format and
into some type of data platform

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:10.130
that will serve it
to your models--

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:12.830
for training, for inference,
for whatever it is

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:13.910
you're going to do.

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:15.900
Well, the tools we
have for big data,

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:18.740
we just have to be to plug
that into the software

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:20.210
and the servers
that you're using.

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:21.751
MARK MANDEL: So now
I'm also curious,

00:21:21.751 --> 00:21:23.510
I think you alluded
earlier, that sort

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:26.600
of the whole aspect
of AI is around sort

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:28.414
of continual learning
and the platform

00:21:28.414 --> 00:21:30.080
learning from itself
as it goes forward.

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:30.870
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:21:30.870 --> 00:21:32.453
MARK MANDEL: What
does that look like?

00:21:32.453 --> 00:21:35.960
Are you just redoing the same
training over and over again,

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:39.260
or is that a slightly
different process as it grows?

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:40.160
How does that work?

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:41.410
MELANIE WARRICK: It can vary.

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:43.520
When you're using some
type of algorithm,

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:46.940
like a Markov algorithm
where the statistics that

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:49.670
are involved in it will
continually sort of adapt based

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:54.470
on real-time readings of
reactions from people,

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:57.290
that would be one way where the
algorithm itself is adapting

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:00.830
as the reactions are coming in
and it's reading it real time.

00:22:00.830 --> 00:22:03.987
Sometimes you do have to do
training offline, or many times

00:22:03.987 --> 00:22:05.570
you have to do
training offline, where

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:08.930
you will pick frequency
of how often you do it

00:22:08.930 --> 00:22:11.000
with the new data
that you've captured.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:12.950
So it can take a couple
of different forms

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:15.530
in terms of real
time versus offline.

00:22:15.530 --> 00:22:17.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
following on the same topic,

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:21.440
since you have algorithms
that continuously adapt

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:24.110
to the conditions and to
the contexts where they're

00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:26.240
executing, does
that mean that you

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:29.720
should be worried about the
algorithms learning something

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:30.559
wrong?

00:22:30.559 --> 00:22:31.600
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh yeah.

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:34.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: By that,
I mean, for instance, you

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:39.090
have a self-driving car that
learns how to drive by itself.

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:40.670
How do you make
sure that it doesn't

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:43.334
learn how to drive
backwards on the highway?

00:22:43.334 --> 00:22:44.750
MELANIE WARRICK:
Human in the mix.

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:49.340
You've got to have some
levels of humans evaluating

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:51.165
the system, as well
as you definitely need

00:22:51.165 --> 00:22:52.040
to put in safeguards.

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:55.010
And yes, this is why I
say never trust the data.

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:57.080
You also don't fully
trust the models.

00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:59.450
You have to constantly be
testing and evaluating.

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:01.310
We've seen examples
of products that

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:05.840
are out there that didn't really
work the way they expected.

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:07.980
I think, frankly,
as an engineer,

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:09.440
you learn really quickly--

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:11.930
you want to believe you can
write this code that you don't

00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:13.670
need to fully
test, but all of it

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:15.260
needs to be tested constantly.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:17.301
And you have to constantly
be thinking about what

00:23:17.301 --> 00:23:18.560
it is you might be missing.

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:20.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
How do you test it?

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:24.200
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, it
depends on what you're doing.

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:25.900
Sometimes you test
it by writing tests.

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:29.570
Sometimes you test it by
exposing it to small samples.

00:23:29.570 --> 00:23:31.029
Sometimes you put
it into the wild,

00:23:31.029 --> 00:23:33.153
and then you find out really
quickly that maybe you

00:23:33.153 --> 00:23:34.940
shouldn't have and
you pull back and then

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:38.372
you hope for better results
the next time you do it.

00:23:38.372 --> 00:23:39.080
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:41.960
I've definitely heard
that, in people's minds,

00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:46.015
people think, oh, I'm going to
sprinkle AI or ML over a thing

00:23:46.015 --> 00:23:48.140
and it's just going to give
me this perfect result.

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:52.550
But it seems like the AI or
the ML might get you 80% there.

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:55.340
And then from there, it's
sort of nudging and scraping

00:23:55.340 --> 00:23:57.110
and crafting until you
get a good product.

00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:59.360
MELANIE WARRICK: Something
I like to stress of people.

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:01.130
You know, it's true with
anything with modeling,

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:02.520
when you're doing anything
with machine learning,

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:04.640
you have to come up
with the right question

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:07.160
or the right problem
you're solving for

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:09.860
before you pick any tools,
before you do anything.

00:24:09.860 --> 00:24:11.810
Then you start to
have to think about,

00:24:11.810 --> 00:24:14.000
what is the data that
I have to do this?

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:16.130
And it doesn't have to
be big data all the time.

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:18.680
People can do
artificial intelligence

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:19.709
with small samples.

00:24:19.709 --> 00:24:20.750
There's research on that.

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:22.660
But you have to
think about the data.

00:24:22.660 --> 00:24:24.320
What's the current
setup of the data?

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:28.160
And then you can
start to do the model.

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:30.830
It takes time to
actually develop anything

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:33.740
and to really see,
is it effective?

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:35.720
And you have to also
ask yourself, what is it

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:37.190
you're trying to achieve for?

00:24:37.190 --> 00:24:40.330
We're typically
evaluating and saying--

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:42.300
accuracy is the easiest
thing to explain.

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:44.450
There's a number of ways
to evaluate these metrics,

00:24:44.450 --> 00:24:48.020
but you have to ask yourself at
what percentage is acceptable?

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:50.030
If it's something
that's life or death,

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:54.200
maybe I need to be
99.9999% accurate on this.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:56.930
If it's something that is
like, we're playing a game

00:24:56.930 --> 00:24:58.910
and it needs to win
most of the time,

00:24:58.910 --> 00:25:01.450
maybe 80% to 90%
accuracy is acceptable.

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:03.200
MARK MANDEL: This is
where I ask questions

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:05.700
based on very vague knowledge.

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:07.223
I believe you can
sort of overtrain

00:25:07.223 --> 00:25:08.473
if you have certain data sets.

00:25:08.473 --> 00:25:09.740
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh yeah.

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:11.448
MARK MANDEL: Can you
explain what that is

00:25:11.448 --> 00:25:13.820
and how to beat that or
hopefully that doesn't happen?

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:15.040
Is there a magic
wand or something?

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:16.745
MELANIE WARRICK: You guys are
asking all the good questions.

00:25:16.745 --> 00:25:17.990
Like, tell us exactly.

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:19.400
And it's interesting,
too, trying

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:22.310
to do this verbally
versus actually having--

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:24.890
I usually love showing images
because it sometimes helps

00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:26.390
kind of hit the point home.

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:29.147
But yes, you can
definitely overfit,

00:25:29.147 --> 00:25:30.230
and you can also underfit.

00:25:30.230 --> 00:25:32.360
So when you overfit,
it means you've

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:35.930
made it so that the perfectly
fits the training data set

00:25:35.930 --> 00:25:37.220
that it's been exposed to.

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:40.300
But it doesn't generalize.

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:43.400
If you show it anything new
that it hasn't seen before,

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:46.370
then it won't be able to
give any kind of prediction

00:25:46.370 --> 00:25:49.730
or classification that
is actually useful

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:52.760
because it's become so
perfectly fit to the data.

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:56.660
When you look at drawing a
line for linear regression

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:59.230
to a data set, that's
a great example.

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:02.284
If you look up what
overfitting looks like for just

00:26:02.284 --> 00:26:04.700
linear regression, I think you
can get a good visual sense

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:06.060
of what that looks like.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.212
And so people put what's
known as regularization

00:26:08.212 --> 00:26:08.920
into your models.

00:26:08.920 --> 00:26:10.150
It makes it kind of fuzzy.

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:12.100
It makes it a
little more general.

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:13.649
And regularization
is extremely key

00:26:13.649 --> 00:26:16.190
when you're doing deep learning
and anything with neural nets

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:19.400
because that's an area
that easily overfits.

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:23.240
So you don't want it to
be too perfectly matched

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:24.740
to the training
set, and you always

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:27.140
want to set aside some
data it's never seen before

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:29.990
to see when you're testing it.

00:26:29.990 --> 00:26:30.949
Does it work and apply?

00:26:30.949 --> 00:26:31.865
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:26:31.865 --> 00:26:34.310
I heard an example that I
thought it was really cool.

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:37.550
I think it was when we did
the episode with Kaggle.

00:26:37.550 --> 00:26:38.975
MARK MANDEL: Oh yeah.

00:26:38.975 --> 00:26:41.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: A very
good example of overfitting

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:43.294
was you tried to predict
the price of houses.

00:26:43.294 --> 00:26:44.210
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:47.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And one of the
houses is let's say $2 million

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.090
and has one broken door.

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:52.940
And you could learn that
whenever any house has

00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:56.090
a broken door, it should be a
$3 million no matter how big it

00:26:56.090 --> 00:26:56.890
is.

00:26:56.890 --> 00:26:59.420
Specific things like, oh
yeah, that's totally wrong.

00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:01.280
So yeah, I really like that.

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:02.617
It's very interesting.

00:27:02.617 --> 00:27:04.950
MELANIE WARRICK: I don't know
the exact details of this,

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:06.530
but I had this example
explained to me once

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:08.630
where, way back in the
day when they were first

00:27:08.630 --> 00:27:11.720
exploring neural nets,
they did this training on I

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:13.220
think it was tanks.

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:18.140
And they were trying to get
recognizing between the US

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:19.020
and Russian tanks.

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:21.710
But the problem is they
trained the actual algorithm

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:23.480
with all these US
tanks that were

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:27.860
taken by really well-done
marketing shots in really

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:30.485
sunny spaces, whereas
the Russian tanks were

00:27:30.485 --> 00:27:33.380
are cloudy and
difficult and grainy.

00:27:33.380 --> 00:27:35.180
When they tried it
out in the real world,

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:37.520
they had it thinking
everything was a tank

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:40.420
because it was grainy and dark.

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:43.630
And it had basically
trained on the clouds

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:46.970
and on the sunshine versus
the actual tank itself.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.964
So anyways, yes,
it's funny when you

00:27:49.964 --> 00:27:51.380
start to dig into
these algorithms

00:27:51.380 --> 00:27:55.070
to see what specifically is it
really focusing on as the way

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:58.040
to say this is my way
to classify or predict.

00:27:58.040 --> 00:28:00.349
And it's very common
that people resist

00:28:00.349 --> 00:28:02.390
using deep learning in
particular because of that

00:28:02.390 --> 00:28:04.310
because it's hard
to fully break into.

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:05.562
And they're working on this.

00:28:05.562 --> 00:28:06.770
It's called interpretability.

00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:08.750
But they're working
on understanding

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:11.944
why is the algorithm picking
the classification that it is.

00:28:11.944 --> 00:28:14.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is
actually super interesting,

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:17.240
and I thought that that
was not a thing at all.

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:19.820
Once you have a neural network,
if you have something that

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:23.250
predicts numbers, somehow
you can see some layers

00:28:23.250 --> 00:28:26.100
inside of a neural network
kind of showing, well,

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.960
this recognizes a circle.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:29.090
So you kind of figure out.

00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:32.030
Is that a thing at higher
scale, like if you're trying

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:33.394
to recognize, is this a cat?

00:28:33.394 --> 00:28:34.310
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:35.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Can you actually

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:36.710
see that in the neural network?

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:36.950
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:38.658
They've got some great
examples out there

00:28:38.658 --> 00:28:42.767
in terms of showing what
are these nets really seeing

00:28:42.767 --> 00:28:43.850
when it looks for the cat.

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:45.391
What are some of
the distinct, what's

00:28:45.391 --> 00:28:47.705
known as features, to
help identify the data

00:28:47.705 --> 00:28:48.590
set in particular?

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:50.540
And there's great images
out there actually

00:28:50.540 --> 00:28:54.560
online that will give you a
perspective on how it focuses.

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:58.040
Classifying a 7 versus a 9,
it will look at that loop

00:28:58.040 --> 00:29:01.190
that the nine will have
that the 7 won't have,

00:29:01.190 --> 00:29:02.840
and that will be
the feature that

00:29:02.840 --> 00:29:04.860
helps it distinguish
between the two numbers

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:06.630
and creates a
higher probability.

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:09.740
MARK MANDEL: So how does it
do that given a neural net

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:11.680
is essentially it's math, right?

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:14.480
So how can you determine
from these numbers

00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:15.370
that there's a loop?

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:16.444
It's looking at the loop?

00:29:16.444 --> 00:29:17.610
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, yeah.

00:29:17.610 --> 00:29:19.610
Now you want me to
explain the math verbally.

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:21.590
This is awesome.

00:29:21.590 --> 00:29:25.970
I mean, the way
it's doing it, it's

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:28.180
basically converting the image.

00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:29.930
So in images in
particular, it converts it

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:31.490
all into numerical values.

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:36.150
And then, based on the
way you do the math,

00:29:36.150 --> 00:29:38.990
it will find a way to
emphasize specifically

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:42.300
the numerical values for
that part of the image.

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:44.030
So the image itself
gets converted

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:45.450
into numerical values.

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:47.360
There's a lot of
math that's going on.

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:50.420
And within that, it's
emphasizing and saying,

00:29:50.420 --> 00:29:52.560
focus on this specific area.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:57.330
If you see this area in
terms of numerical values--

00:29:57.330 --> 00:30:00.590
let's say it's all
grayscale, and 1s and 0s--

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:02.230
I'll just make it
really simple--

00:30:02.230 --> 00:30:05.140
are focused in the specific
area of the picture.

00:30:05.140 --> 00:30:08.170
Then if you see
that, then that's

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:11.380
a high probability
it's going to be a 9.

00:30:11.380 --> 00:30:12.370
So that certain ways.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:14.890
And how you actually see
that in the image itself

00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:16.540
or how you see that
in the neural net

00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:22.000
is ways that we take the actual
data set inside of the net

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:23.129
and visualize it.

00:30:23.129 --> 00:30:25.420
I'm going to say that's my
best way of explaining that,

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:27.970
frankly, right now,
but you find ways

00:30:27.970 --> 00:30:31.150
to surface it and
visualize and convert

00:30:31.150 --> 00:30:33.820
what the actual model looks
like into some type of--

00:30:33.820 --> 00:30:36.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I see
that as almost like debugging

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:39.414
a program, where you're actually
see the value of the variables

00:30:39.414 --> 00:30:40.330
when you're executing.

00:30:40.330 --> 00:30:42.542
You can kind of
see, oh, these are

00:30:42.542 --> 00:30:45.000
the pieces that are activating
because [? it sees ?] around

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:46.180
or whatever.

00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:47.620
Does that mean
that, whenever you

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:54.280
train a big neural network that
is able to recognize circles

00:30:54.280 --> 00:30:57.130
and lines and
whatever, you could

00:30:57.130 --> 00:31:00.144
reuse that part to then
train on something else.

00:31:00.144 --> 00:31:01.810
Like, for instance,
let's say no, you're

00:31:01.810 --> 00:31:04.810
going to try to
recognize diagrams

00:31:04.810 --> 00:31:05.860
or something like that.

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:07.780
MELANIE WARRICK: So you are
bringing up transfer learning,

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:09.489
and that's become a
really popular thing.

00:31:09.489 --> 00:31:11.863
A couple of things I'll say
about this around neural nets

00:31:11.863 --> 00:31:12.610
in particular.

00:31:12.610 --> 00:31:15.670
Neural nets came to popularity
recently in the last few years

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:17.950
because the computation
power was there

00:31:17.950 --> 00:31:20.050
to actually do these
computations you have to do,

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:22.330
the intensive computation
you have to do for it,

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:24.430
and the data was available.

00:31:24.430 --> 00:31:27.610
What was initially popular
for neural nets was feature

00:31:27.610 --> 00:31:29.590
engineering, feature
engineering meaning

00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:32.110
that you can run data
through it and you

00:31:32.110 --> 00:31:36.100
identify the primary important
parts of the data that

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:37.744
will help you build the model.

00:31:37.744 --> 00:31:39.910
And that's something that
people had to do manually,

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:41.743
and it was really
intensive for a long time.

00:31:41.743 --> 00:31:44.140
But using a neural net
made it a lot easier.

00:31:44.140 --> 00:31:46.360
Now, people are getting
excited about what's known

00:31:46.360 --> 00:31:48.070
as transfer learning, where--

00:31:48.070 --> 00:31:50.830
more so with neural nets--
because you've already

00:31:50.830 --> 00:31:53.230
got trained models that
have been successful,

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:56.350
and they're good at some
type of image classification.

00:31:56.350 --> 00:31:58.540
And you can take
parts of that model

00:31:58.540 --> 00:32:01.570
and apply it to a different
image classification problem,

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:04.989
and it's quick to train, quick
to apply, quick to adapt.

00:32:04.989 --> 00:32:05.530
So it's cool.

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:07.630
It's like figuring
out a cat and a dog,

00:32:07.630 --> 00:32:11.230
and it gets used for figuring
out trees or something.

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:14.050
MARK MANDEL: That's
very human in that way.

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:17.050
Like I've done sport X, and
I used to do like one thing,

00:32:17.050 --> 00:32:18.960
and then I can do
exactly the same thing.

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:20.200
You took basketball
skills, took it over

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:22.010
to something like netball
or something like that.

00:32:22.010 --> 00:32:22.360
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:24.220
MARK MANDEL: That's
kind of super cool.

00:32:24.220 --> 00:32:27.970
So when is the robot
