00:37:47.960 --> 00:37:51.160
Our question of the week
is about Kubernetes.

00:37:51.160 --> 00:37:53.090
So in Kubernetes, you have pods.

00:37:53.090 --> 00:37:55.550
And those pods,
whenever they die--

00:37:55.550 --> 00:37:58.790
they are actually pretty
short-lived sometimes--

00:37:58.790 --> 00:38:02.370
and whenever one dies, if
you have a service that says,

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:05.130
well, I should have let's
say 10 instances of it,

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:06.560
a new one is created.

00:38:06.560 --> 00:38:08.960
And you don't really
have control on that.

00:38:08.960 --> 00:38:12.250
Whenever a pod dies,
a new one is created.

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:16.840
Now, how would you do something
in between those actions?

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:20.250
Meaning like, if I want to
do something at the moment

00:38:20.250 --> 00:38:23.120
a new pod is created,
how do we do that?

00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:25.730
And if I want to do
something the moment

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:29.840
a pod is killed or is
detected or considered

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:31.460
to be dead, how do you do that?

00:38:31.460 --> 00:38:32.210
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:34.910
So this is actually
something that

00:38:34.910 --> 00:38:37.490
gets me kind of very
excited about Kubernetes.

00:38:37.490 --> 00:38:40.940
So Kubernetes has a
whole REST API as well as

00:38:40.940 --> 00:38:44.390
a series of clients for most
languages, like Go and Python

00:38:44.390 --> 00:38:45.564
and other ones as well.

00:38:45.564 --> 00:38:47.480
And what makes me get
excited about Kubernetes

00:38:47.480 --> 00:38:50.060
is that Kubernetes is
this amazing thing that

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:53.436
does a lot of really cool
work in terms of deployments

00:38:53.436 --> 00:38:54.810
and services and
stuff like that,

00:38:54.810 --> 00:38:56.990
but it also gives you so
much of the building blocks

00:38:56.990 --> 00:38:58.569
and the APIs to
make it do all sorts

00:38:58.569 --> 00:39:00.360
of other interesting
stuff programmatically

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:01.280
so you can really control it.

00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:02.696
So one of those
things that it has

00:39:02.696 --> 00:39:06.355
is basically a whole watch API.

00:39:06.355 --> 00:39:07.730
Most of the clients
wrap that up.

00:39:07.730 --> 00:39:10.820
You can access it also directly
through the REST API as well.

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:14.874
You can basically say, hey, tell
me whenever this stuff happens.

00:39:14.874 --> 00:39:16.790
So for example, what
we're talking about here,

00:39:16.790 --> 00:39:19.854
hey, tell me whenever an
event happens for a pod.

00:39:19.854 --> 00:39:22.020
And you can actually apply
filters to those as well,

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:23.550
and you can do fun
stuff like that.

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:25.175
And what's cool about
that then is then

00:39:25.175 --> 00:39:27.490
I can just have maybe a
deployment that happens.

00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:28.767
It sits on Kubernetes.

00:39:28.767 --> 00:39:31.100
It can connect to the Kubernetes
API through the client.

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:33.012
And it can say, hey,
tell me whenever

00:39:33.012 --> 00:39:34.220
something happens with a pod.

00:39:34.220 --> 00:39:35.990
And so when one gets created,
it will get a message

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:37.073
saying something happened.

00:39:37.073 --> 00:39:39.772
When one gets deleted, it gets
a message, something happened.

00:39:39.772 --> 00:39:42.230
And then programmatically, you
could either tell Kubernetes

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:44.960
to do something else, maybe
fire off a message to a log,

00:39:44.960 --> 00:39:46.880
do some analytics or
some sort of metrics.

00:39:46.880 --> 00:39:49.379
There's all sorts of fun stuff
you could definitely do here.

00:39:49.379 --> 00:39:52.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So does that
mean that you do a request

00:39:52.820 --> 00:39:55.250
and you give some
kind of callback

00:39:55.250 --> 00:39:58.280
that it will be
called, like a webhook?

00:39:58.280 --> 00:40:01.010
Or is it like a long thing
and you just get new messages

00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:02.760
from that connection?

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:06.040
MARK MANDEL: So it depends on
your client and how it does it.

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:07.420
So I usually use the Go client.

00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:09.753
So what it normally does is
it hands you back a channel,

00:40:09.753 --> 00:40:11.587
and then data gets
pushed into that channel.

00:40:11.587 --> 00:40:13.252
If people aren't
familiar with channels,

00:40:13.252 --> 00:40:15.240
think of it basically as
like a blocking queue.

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:17.614
And then data goes into that,
and then I can just pick up

00:40:17.614 --> 00:40:18.810
that as it comes through.

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:18.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:40:18.910 --> 00:40:20.940
MARK MANDEL: And then I can do
a Go routine, so basically fork

00:40:20.940 --> 00:40:23.023
off another thread and
keep on running from there.

00:40:23.023 --> 00:40:25.500
And it's really
straightforward, and I

00:40:25.500 --> 00:40:28.470
don't have to worry about
starting processes or killing

